you would hardly notice it if you drove  by but this warehouse near downtown  ottawa  houses nothing less than the very  machinery of canada's democracy  forms manuals ballots  all that is needed to record the  political preferences of up to 19  million voters  in 338 ridings but the work here these  days  is different the pandemic changes  everything  and it forced elections canada to  rethink the entire process of  sending canadians to the polls  natasha gautier speaks for elections  canada and for more than a year  she and her colleagues have been focused  on one thing  to make sure that if there is an  election we are able to deliver an  election that is as  safe as possible for voters  for election canada's staff for poll  workers because that's also a priority  for us  and to make sure that we are able to do  that in a way that the integrity of the  election  is not compromised in any way it starts  with  safety elections canada has made a huge  buy of ppe  and it's all here waiting to be shipped  to hundreds of polling stations  19 million surgical masks gloves  hand sanitizer half a million face  shields  plexiglas barriers and then there's the  pencils  in a pandemic election no pencil will be  used  twice so elections canada has purchased  lots of single-use pencils 16 million of  them  the pencil is vital because for all of  our history  this is the way we've picked our  politicians a piece of paper  a pencil and an ex that's how people  will vote in the next general election  even if it's held in a time of pandemic  there won't be any e-voting no digital  ballots no using an iphone to cast an x  it will be pencil and paper but there  may be a change to  where and when you use that pencil and  paper because  here at elections canada they believe  that as many as five million people  will vote by mail if we have an election  in a pandemic  and in 2019 in the last election just  fifty four thousand voted by mail  but now this year that could be five  million  how about another number we need only  look to the provincial pandemic  elections in british columbia  saskatchewan new brunswick  newfoundland and labrador and of course  to the u.s presidential election to see  how the pandemic  changed the way voters pick their  politicians how politicians pick their  voters  and how officials make sure it's all  done fairly and accurately  bob doer ran the winning ndp war room  for the bc provincial election last fall  we were extremely nervous because  there's no playbook on this  there's there's no playbook on how you  run a pandemic campaign  pollster dimitri pantazopoulos was  inside the losing war room with the bc  liberals  there are no big rallies there's you  know it makes it very difficult to  generate momentum  the big difference how each of those  campaigns dealt with  mail-in ballots when we identified  people that were going to vote for us  to make sure that they were able to  register to get a mail-in ballot that  was their first objective  the second objective once they got the  mail-in ballot  was to how to fill it out people a lot  of people have never done this before  the mail out ballot is a little bit of a  different uh a different  species you know first of all  encouraging people to actually  get their ballot and then secondly  focusing on how to get them to return it  the ndp literally had to show its  digital first  millennial supporters how to work an  envelope  a lot of people were not uh we're not  used to going and  seeing that red box at the end of their  uh at the end of the street and putting  a  mail in there and voila it goes  somewhere i've written in  the name of who i want to vote for in a  federal pandemic election  expect parties to spend a lot of time  teaching supporters  how to use the mail-in ballot so this is  the package  so this is the package this is what we  call a  special ballot kit so each envelope will  have a number  and while asking for a mail-in ballot  will be done online  everything else is all analog paper  pencil and the old-fashioned mail system  voters will have to follow the  instructions that come with the ballot  or risk their ballot getting thrown out  each ballot  envelope will be open by hand one by one  no machines and each will be counted by  at least two  poll workers again no machines people  should be prepared to expect delays in  the results  and we may not know the final results of  the election on election night  while the act of voting and counting the  votes is a paper-based  human activity with almost no machines  involved  it will be just the opposite for the act  of campaigning  the smartphone will play a starring role  in a pandemic election  it really changes how you're able to  to to find people and actually also get  them out to vote  using technology to connect voters to  politicians  has been a growing trend for decades  it's a trend university of guelph  political scientist  tamara small has been studying she says  more technology means voters will get an  individualized experience  algorithms will slice and dice  demographic data putting voters into  smaller and smaller groups and  that changes the democratic process  this idea of a one-on-one campaign why i  think it's seductive i also think  there's some real democratic issues that  we have to  sort of think through voters may not  even be aware of the pitches  a campaign is making to other kinds of  voters i actually don't know whether or  not  my instagram ads are the same as my  neighbor's instagram ad  and probably they're not and in any  event an  old digital campaign may leave some  voters behind  it's easy to think that technology is  easy it's evenly distributed to  everybody it's just not  and so therefore some people are not  going to have the same sort of campaign  as other people might  political rallies coffee shop arguments  door step  discussions all of that disappears in a  pandemic election  we did not allow volunteers into our  campaign office it was just paid staff  and we did no door-to-door canvassing  two big things that we've never done  before  and that lack of social interaction with  campaigners with other voters  is likely to result in lower voter  turnout  it's interesting to have people come to  your door that's a good thing that that  usually gets people out to vote  and we're to lose a number of those  things low voter turnout high voter  turnout  elections canada is ready for all  scenarios  i'm very proud of the work that my  colleagues have done  and when the 44th general election is  all over  all the millions of ballots cast mail in  or otherwise  we'll come back here back to the  warehouse at elections canada  to be stored for 10 years one  final safeguard against challenges a  complete  paper record of the pandemic election
 

